    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "Both directions of both laws are asserted as a single conjunction, so one unsat settles all four implications. Only three of the four implications are intuitionistically valid -- `not (p and q) -> (not p) or (not q)` is not -- so the `classical-two-valued-bool-semantics` entry in the axiom footprint is load-bearing here, not an artifact of the method. This is the pair of identities the CNF front end relies on when it pushes negations to the leaves before Tseitin encoding, which is why it is worth pinning as a fact rather than assuming.\n\nOn the axiom footprint: it names the trust base of the SMT ROUTE that established this fact -- the axeyum-ir Boolean evaluator, and the two-valued semantics of the SMT-LIB Bool sort that makes exhaustive truth-table enumeration a proof. It is deliberately NOT empty and deliberately NOT a Lean axiom list. Nothing here went through axeyum's Lean kernel. Measured 2026-08-14 with `cargo run -q -p axeyum-lean-kernel --example nat_axiom_inventory`: the `logic` prelude reports axiom=0 opaque=0 quotient=0, and its declarations are exactly True, False, And, Or, Iff, Not (as `a -> False`), Eq and Exists -- an intuitionistic core with no `Classical.em`, no `propext` and no `Quot.sound`. Zero trusted declarations there means the kernel assumes nothing, not that it can prove everything.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
        "epoch": {
          "commit": "d637d83f77dbfa43c98eee4ab0ad78d235099006",
          "source": "commit",
          "unix": 1787144076
        },
